// The smallest whole program: blank the screen, print, return. The screen
// and text packages resolve per target — @8bitscript/pet/text on a PET
// build, @8bitscript/web/text on a web build — and each writes its own
// screen the way that machine wants; this file does not know which, which
// is the point of it.
//
// It carries no media. Both kinds need a frame the program does not have:
// `audio.play()` arms a voice and it is `audio.update()`, counted down over
// the following frames, that releases it again — so a single call before
// returning to BASIC leaves the voice gated on and the machine sounding it
// after the program is over (measured on the C64). `graphics.place()` is
// the same shape: on every machine but the VIC-20 it records where an
// object goes and leaves the drawing to the next update, which never
// comes, so the object costs its whole pipeline and shows nothing —
// measured here at 1587 bytes on the PET against 108 without it.
//
// Media belongs to the examples that have a frame loop to drive it:
// `fancy`, `swarm`, `joystick` and `media-walk` all call `audio.update()`
// inside `waitFrame()`, which is the shape that both sounds and stops. The
// smallest whole program is this one, and it is small.
import { screen } from "@8bitscript/screen";
import { text } from "@8bitscript/text";

export function main(): void {
    screen.blank();
    text.print(0, "Hello World!");
    text.releaseCursor();
}
